{"task":"Take down the installation being replaced, and stop believing an open socket","goals":"Make ldev handle the migration itself instead of leaving four root-owned commands to the reader","status":"Waiting","recorded_at":"2026-09-10T02:16:16Z","body":"Installing ldev over an existing setup left both live. The install wrote its config,\ninstalled its daemon and reported success, while the previous topology kept running: one\nserver held 80, another held 443, and the bare https://<tld>/ URL answered from neither.\nEvery step had worked; the machine was broken. Cleaning that up was four root-owned\ncommands the reader had to know to run, which is not something an installer gets to leave\nto a README.\n\nIt now finds what it is replacing and offers to take it down.\n\nJobs are matched by CONTENT, never by filename: a launchd job counts because its\nProgramArguments run caddy against a Caddyfile under the sites or config directory, not\nbecause it is named something ldev-ish. The one that motivated this is called\ncom.bryce.caddy-matsu — nothing in that name says ldev, or caddy's role, or which TLD it\nserves, and a filename convention is not something to assume about a file someone else\nwrote.\n\nOnly jobs wanting port 80 or 443 are offered for removal. This is the distinction that\nmatters, because the wrong answer deletes a working site's server: a per-site Caddy on\n8443 is not in the way, it is about to be FRONTED by the wildcard one, and stopping it\nwould take down the very site the install is meant to make reachable. On this machine that\nis the difference between com.bryce.caddy-matsu (443, in the way) and\ncom.bryce.caddy-seasonal-drops (8443, kept) — and the first draft of this got it wrong and\nwould have removed both.\n\nWhich port a job wants is read from its Caddyfile, per ADDRESS rather than per line. A\nCaddy address line can carry several and each is independent, so in\n\n    matsu.ldev, matsu-dev.ldev:8444 {\n\nthe neighbour is on 8444 and matsu.ldev is on the default 443. Reading the first port on\nthe line calls that job harmless and leaves it holding the port ldev needs — the same trap\nbin/ldev already documents for its own address parsing, hit again from a different\ndirection.\n\nThree things deliberately not done: site folders are never touched; a busy port with no\nlaunchd job behind it is named rather than guessed at, because that is somebody's\n`caddy run` in a terminal and not the installer's to kill; and lsof is guarded, since it\nexits non-zero when it can see nothing — including the root-owned socket that is exactly\nthe case here — and an unguarded lsof pipeline under `set -e` with pipefail killed an\nearlier version of this check on any machine with something already on port 80.\n\nlaunchctl enable now runs on both sides of a daemon's life. launchd keeps a per-label\ndisabled record that outlives bootout AND deleting the plist, and a later bootstrap then\nfails with \"Bootstrap failed: 5: Input/output error\" — naming neither the label nor the\nword disabled, so it reads as a broken plist. Enabling before bootstrap is a no-op when\nthere is no such record and the fix when there is; enabling on removal leaves the label\nclean for whoever comes next.\n\ndoctor gained the two checks that would have caught this from the other end:\n\n  - An open socket on 443 is not proof it is OURS. Believing it is how the broken layer\n    came back green: 443 was held by a daemon serving exactly one host, that host loaded,\n    every other name aborted the handshake, and the browser said ERR_SSL_PROTOCOL_ERROR —\n    naming neither the port nor the server nor the missing site. doctor now reports a\n    listener on 443 with no ldev LaunchDaemon behind it as a failure, and points at the\n    installer.\n  - The CA is checked in the trust store rather than in a log. The daemon cannot install\n    it: running as root under launchd it has no tty for the sudo that needs, so it logs\n    \"failed to install root certificate\" once and carries on serving. Worth noting the\n    plist already sets HOME and XDG_DATA_HOME to the user's directory, so the daemon and\n    `caddy trust` share one CA — which is why this is noise when the CA is already trusted\n    and a warning on every site at once when it is not. Only the trust store distinguishes\n    those two, and this machine turned out to be the first.\n\ntest/existing-install.test.sh asserts the classification against real plist + Caddyfile\npairs, including the mixed-address trap, :443 named outright, http:// with and without a\nport, a keyless global block not being mistaken for an address, and a plist whose config is\nmissing not being assumed to be in the way.\n\nAlso corrected in the README: the Install section still said the installer asks four\nthings including a serving mode, which survived the merge that removed modes.\n","parent_commit":"6f9e40077e3500c6e7753de259891799669b5426","repo":"brave-cedar-fb33","pct":"82","tasks":[{"id":"find","content":"Find launchd jobs serving the TLD by content, not filename","status":"completed"},{"id":"classify","content":"Offer only jobs wanting 80/443; keep per-site servers on high ports, parsed per address","status":"completed"},{"id":"takedown","content":"Bootout, enable the label, delete the plist, then wait for the ports to free","status":"completed"},{"id":"enable","content":"launchctl enable before bootstrap, so a stale disabled record cannot fail the install","status":"completed"},{"id":"lsof","content":"Guard lsof, which exits non-zero on a root-owned socket it cannot see","status":"completed"},{"id":"doctor-443","content":"doctor: a listener on 443 with no ldev LaunchDaemon behind it is a failure","status":"completed"},{"id":"doctor-ca","content":"doctor: check the trust store for Caddy's CA rather than a log nobody opens","status":"completed"},{"id":"tests","content":"test/existing-install.test.sh — 8 assertions over real plist + Caddyfile pairs","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-10T01:14:35.832Z","status_at":"2026-09-10T01:14:35.832Z"},{"id":"docs","content":"README: Replacing an existing setup, the disclosure row, the ERR_SSL_PROTOCOL_ERROR symptom, and the stale serving-mode line","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:08:36.006Z","status_at":"2026-09-09T19:22:30.481Z"},{"id":"rehome-run","content":"Run ldev rehome against the three real WordPress sites","status":"pending","human_reason":"Rewrites live site data: 16,224 database rows for seasonal-drops alone, plus WP_HOME/WP_SITEURL in wp-config.php. The command refuses to run without a terminal to confirm on, so an agent cannot execute it and should not be given --yes on someone's real databases.","created_at":"2026-09-09T19:22:30.481Z","status_at":"2026-09-09T19:22:30.481Z"},{"id":"migrate","content":"Run the new installer on this machine to replace com.bryce.caddy-matsu with the wildcard daemon","status":"pending","human_reason":"Root-owned and briefly takes matsu.ldev offline while 443 changes hands. The installer now does the takedown itself, but starting it is Bryce's call, not something to trigger unasked on a working machine."}],"agent":"claude-code","changelog":"Unreviewed (agent unavailable).","code_quality":"Unreviewed (agent unavailable).","vulnerabilities":"Unreviewed (agent unavailable).","code_style_suggestions":"Unreviewed (agent unavailable).","reusability":"Unreviewed (agent unavailable).","system_compatibility":"Unreviewed (agent unavailable).","sessions":[{"provider":"claude","id":"3e8f56a2-7837-42a5-adfb-4e072f3876ee","title":"https://sites.ldev/ (or any other name I put) doesn't auto redirect to the projects dashboard","linked_at":"2026-09-10T02:16:16Z"}],"review_run":{"state":"did_not_run","at":"2026-09-10T02:16:16Z","attempts":1,"reason":"Review skipped at the caller's request (--no-review / automatedReview: false)."}}
